irst used in the 1992 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":8},"c108":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2000 census, which left Maryland with 8 seats. It was first used in the 2002 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":8},"c113":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2010 census, which left Maryland with 8 seats. It was first used in the 2012 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":8},"c115":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Maryland's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 2016 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":6},"c118":{"why":"event","kind":"court","text":"New map following: State court strikes the 2021 map. In Szeliga v. Lamone, a Maryland circuit court ruled in March 2022 that the legislature's 2021 congressional map violated the state constitution. The legislature passed a new map days later, signed into law on April 4, 2022, and used from that year's election.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/maryland/"],"districtsChanged":8}}}